 r"I am writing to apply for the 2027 Chase Digital Development Programme -- Full-time Analyst role in London. I completed an MSc in Health Data Science at University College London in September 2026, where a distinction is expected, after a BSc in Biological Sciences at Imperial College London, and I currently work as a Data Analyst Intern at TikTok Shop UK. Across my academic and professional experience I have worked closely with users and engineers, measured whether new features actually worked, and turned unclear problems into products people use.",

 r"What attracts me most to this programme is that the work has a visible end point: digital banking experiences that millions of customers use every day. The three rotations across product, working with developers, engineers and the customer experience team, are exactly how I would like to learn product management, by seeing how an intuitive experience is built from start to finish. I enjoy taking an unclear business problem, identifying what users actually need, and turning it into a workflow that can be tested and improved.",

 r"At TikTok Shop UK I analyse merchant and transaction data in SQL and Python and build the KPI frameworks and dashboards that operations, product and commercial teams use to set targets and track initiatives. When my supervisor asked me to configure a merchant campaign by hand, I worked out the logic and proposed a different approach: an AI agent built with Claude Code, with a validation step to check its accuracy. My supervisor was sceptical at first, but I delivered it before the same-day deadline and it cut the time the task took by around 40 to 50 percent. It taught me that a new tool only succeeds when the people using it trust it.",

 r"At DS Digital Technology I worked as a Data Scientist Intern on a patient-facing AI product for a pharmaceutical client. I worked with the client's clinical experts to define what a safe and helpful answer looked like, turned this into a benchmark that the client adopted as its acceptance standard, and designed blinded A/B tests to check whether a new knowledge-graph feature genuinely improved the answers. This was my first real experience of product thinking: agree what good looks like for the user, then test whether a change delivers it.",

